- First iteration: `alias=v`, `main=verbose`. Since `out.v = false` and `out.verbose = true`, the first check `if (out.v !== undefined && out.verbose === undefined)` is false, and the second check `if (out.verbose !== undefined && out.v === undefined)` is also false (because `out.v` is defined).


- The real problem is that negated flags get applied after propagation, so when `--no-v` is processed, `out.v` becomes `false`, but by then the propagation has already run and won't sync this change back to `verbose`. I need to check whether defaults are actually included in the parseArgs output or if they're applied separately.

Looking at line 85, defaults are passed into the parseArgs options, so the parsed result should include `{verbose: true}` from the default. This means `out.verbose` gets set to `true` at line 141-142. Then the negated flag `--no-v` sets `out.v = false` at line 146-148. When propagation runs at lines 151-158, both `out.verbose` and `out.v` are already defined, so neither condition triggers and the mismatch persists.

The solution is to apply negated flags after defaults propagate but before the general propagation logic, and ensure negation works bidirectionally—if negating an alias, also negate its main option, and vice versa.

